      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **State of Matter:** Water is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. The Ecological View (Earth’s Lifeblood)\n* **Global Coverage:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop called the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from the earth, forms clouds, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows into bodies of water, repeating the process.\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Water has a unique chemical structure that allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital minerals and nutrients through ecosystems, our bloodstreams, and the soil.\n\n### 3. The Biological View (The Essence of Life)\n* **Life-Giving:** All known forms of life depend on water. It provides the medium in which the metabolic and cellular processes of organisms take place.\n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects organs and tissues.\n\n### Unique Properties of Water\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n* **Ice Floats:** Unlike most substances, which become denser when they freeze, water expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats on water, allowing aquatic life to survive under frozen lakes during the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n*"
